--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HOW "v3" WAS GRADED, AND WHY NOT FROM A CONFIG LINE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The case is titled VRRP(**v3**). There is NO `version` command on this platform:

    awplus(config-router)# version ?
    % Unrecognized command                              [work/s2b-qmark-router.log]

and the guide's own "VRRPv2 to VRRPv3 transition configuration" chapter upgrades
a router by **changing its software release and rebooting**, not by a command.
So "v3" cannot be read off the configuration, and was graded three ways on the
wire and from the device's own state:
  - the VERSION FIELD of the advertisement, decoded from a capture;
  - the presence of `transition-mode {true|false}` and `alternate-checksum-mode`,
    both of which exist only because VRRPv3 does;
  - `Accept mode: TRUE` by default, which the guide states is specifically the
    AlliedWare Plus **VRRPv3** behaviour (guide p.3).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TOPOLOGY, AND THE ONE DESIGN CHOICE THAT CARRIES THE WHOLE CASE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The group-D bench setup (queue file, committed b0bc177) extends `vlan 10` to
swi_b over the `port1.0.16` trunk, so the DUT, swi_b and host `eth3` are all in
one broadcast domain:

   host eth3 10.38.22.33/27  --(DUT port1.0.10, access vlan 10)--  swi_d  10.38.22.34/27
                                                                     ||
                                   port1.0.16 trunk, tagged vlan 10 + 61
                                                                     ||
   host eth4 10.38.22.65/27  --(DUT port1.0.48, access vlan 48)--  swi_b  10.38.22.35/27
                               DUT vlan48 10.38.22.66/27           + ip route 10.38.22.64/27 10.38.22.34

  VRID 10  =>  virtual MAC **00:00:5e:00:01:0a**   (0000.5e00.01<vrid>, vrid 10 = 0x0a)
  virtual IP 10.38.22.36, **owned by NEITHER router**, so there is no preferred
  master, the election is decided purely by priority, and the virtual MAC is
  used in every case. DUT priority 150 (Master), swi_b priority 100 (Backup).
  10.38.22.36 was confirmed free before use: see LEG V0.

** THE DESIGN CHOICE: swi_b has a static route to 10.38.22.64/27 via the DUT. **
Both host NICs land on the DUT, so when swi_b is Master a frame addressed to the
virtual MAC must HAIRPIN -- host -> swi_b -> DUT -> eth4. That is deliberate,
because it makes the two routers distinguishable **in the dataplane**:

    DUT is Master   -> ONE routing hop   -> the frame arrives at eth4 with TTL 63
    swi_b is Master -> TWO routing hops  -> it arrives with TTL 62

Both routers answer with the SAME virtual MAC and the frames are otherwise
byte-identical, so the TTL is the only field on the wire that says which router
forwarded. That is why `tools/arpleg.py` was extended with `--show-ttl` for this
case (and with `%L2DST`, below).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE MEASUREMENT METHOD
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every counted leg is one `tools/arpleg.py` invocation carrying THREE UDP flows to
the same destination 10.38.22.65, differing ONLY in their Ethernet destination,
plus an ARP request for the virtual IP:

    --arp 10.38.22.36
    --udp '10.38.22.65:9999%00:00:5e:00:01:0a'   SUBJECT    the virtual MAC
          '10.38.22.65:9998%02:00:00:00:00:99'   CONTROL    a MAC nobody owns
          '10.38.22.65:9997%e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43'   INSTRUMENT the DUT's real SVI MAC
    --count 5 --show-ttl --iface eth3 --watch eth4 --sender-ip 10.38.22.33

  - the CONTROL must never arrive. If it does, the switch is flooding rather than
    routing and the subject's arrival would mean nothing.
  - the INSTRUMENT must always arrive, whatever VRRP is doing, because it is
    addressed to the DUT's own permanent SVI MAC. It is what makes a zero on the
    subject a measurement instead of a dead capture.
  - the three flows use three different UDP destination ports so they are
    separable in one capture.

TOOL WORK DONE FOR THIS CASE (generic, in `tools/`, with README entries):
  - `arpleg.py --udp DST[:PORT][@SRC]**%L2DST**` -- a PER-SPEC Ethernet
    destination. `--l2-dst` applies to every `--udp` spec and therefore cannot
    carry a subject and a control that differ only in their link-layer address,
    which is exactly what a first-hop-redundancy case needs.
  - `arpleg.py --show-ttl` -- appends the received IPv4 TTL to each flow.
  Both were self-tested in LEG V0 below, in a real leg with a known-good control.

