LEG V2 -- THE VIRTUAL MAC IS THE SUBJECT, AND IT IS NOT EITHER ROUTER'S  [PASS]
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  COUNTED LEG V1-dut-master, which ARPs for all THREE addresses at once so the
  virtual MAC is discriminated against both real ones in the SAME capture:
                                     [cap V1-dut-master-eth3.pcap / -eth4.pcap]
    eth3:
      x5  ARP-REQ  who-has 10.38.22.36 tell 10.38.22.33
      x5  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.36 is-at **00:00:5e:00:01:0a**
                                   [eth 00:00:5e:00:01:0a -> 00:11:22:33:44:01]
      x5  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.34 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43   (the DUT's real SVI MAC)
      x5  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.35 is-at 00:00:cd:42:30:29   (swi_b's real MAC)
      x14 IP p112 10.38.22.34 -> 224.0.0.18 ttl=255 [eth 00:00:5e:00:01:0a -> ...]
    eth4:
      x5  UDP ...:9999 [ours] **ttl=63**   SUBJECT    via the virtual MAC, ONE hop
      x5  UDP ...:9997 [ours] ttl=63       INSTRUMENT
      x0  UDP ...:9998                     CONTROL
  => the ARP reply for the virtual IP carries 0000.5e00.010a in BOTH the ARP
     payload AND the Ethernet source, and the same capture proves that address is
     neither router's own. That matters here specifically because every SVI on
     this DUT shares one MAC (x960.md 6.5) -- the virtual MAC is the only
     address on this segment that is not e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43.
     Compare LEG V0: the identical ARP request got NO reply at all.

