================================================================================
F-17 (CANDIDATE FINDING, WANTS A DEVELOPER) -- THE DEFAULT VRRPv3-FOR-IPv4
      ADVERTISEMENT CHECKSUM IS NOT THE ONE THE DEVICE'S OWN tcpdump ACCEPTS,
      AND A PAIR WITH MISMATCHED CHECKSUM MODES PRODUCES **TWO MASTERS**
================================================================================
This started as `(bad vrrp cksum 1846)` in LEG V1 and was NOT written off as a
decoder artefact. It was measured.

THE A/B, IN ONE CAPTURE, ONE DECODER, ONE WINDOW   [work/s8b-altcksum-wire-u2.log]
  `alternate-checksum-mode` set on the **DUT only**:
    10.38.22.34  (DUT,   alternate-checksum-mode ON)   VRRPv3 ... prio 150
                                                       ** no checksum complaint **
    10.38.22.35  (swi_b, default mode)                 VRRPv3 ... prio 100
                                                       ** (bad vrrp cksum 4a46) **
  Two frames, same instrument, same second, differing only in which checksum
  style the sender used. The one WITH `alternate-checksum-mode` validates clean.
  => the validator and the DEFAULT mode disagree; the validator and the
     ALTERNATE mode agree.

A THIRD, INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION, from the transition-mode capture
                                            [work/s16c-transition-wire-u2.log]
  With transition mode on, the SAME daemon emits a v2 and a v3 advertisement
  26 microseconds apart. The **VRRPv2** frame validates clean; the **VRRPv3**
  frame in the same pair does not. So the instrument is not simply broken for
  VRRP -- it is specifically the v3 checksum it rejects.

THE SPLIT-MASTER, MEASURED  [work/s8c-swib-split-u3.log, cap V3-split-master-*]
  With the modes mismatched, swi_b **stopped accepting the DUT's advertisements
  and took over**, although it is priority 100 against the DUT's 150:
    DUT   19:03:19  vlan10 Grp 10 Prio 150 **Master**  Master address: 10.38.22.34
    swi_b 19:03:32  vlan10 Grp 10 Prio 100 **Master**  Master address: 10.38.22.35
  A priority-100 router cannot displace a priority-150 router it can hear, so
  this is a receive-side rejection, and the checksum is the only thing that
  changed. The dataplane harm, counted:   [cap V3-split-master-eth3/-eth4.pcap]
    x11 IP p112 **10.38.22.35** -> 224.0.0.18 [eth 00:00:5e:00:01:0a -> ...]
    x10 IP p112 **10.38.22.34** -> 224.0.0.18 [eth 00:00:5e:00:01:0a -> ...]
        ** both routers advertising for vrid 10 from the SAME virtual MAC **
    x10 ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.36 is-at 00:00:5e:00:01:0a
        ** TEN replies to FIVE requests -- both routers answered, and the client
           is now steered by whichever reply lands last **
    eth4: x5 ...:9999 ttl=63   -- the frames still got through, the DUT winning
                                  the L2 race for them; which router serves the
                                  client is a race, not a decision
  Two ports claiming one unicast MAC on one segment is the classic duplicate-MAC
  hazard, and `show vrrp` on each device looks perfectly healthy in isolation.

THE CONTROLS THAT BOUND IT, both measured, so the finding is not over-claimed
  - DEFAULT-to-DEFAULT IS FINE. Before any of this, with both devices in the
    default mode, swi_b read `State: AdminUp - Backup` **and**
    `Master address: 10.38.22.34`         [work/s7b-swib-accepts-u3.log].
    It can only know the master's address by receiving, validating and parsing
    the DUT's advertisements. So AW+-to-AW+ interop in the default is unaffected
    -- both ends compute the checksum the same way. This is NOT a defect that
    appears in a normal single-vendor deployment.
  - ALTERNATE-to-ALTERNATE IS ALSO FINE. Setting `alternate-checksum-mode` on
    swi_b as well re-converged the pair immediately: swi_b back to
    `Backup`, `Master address: 10.38.22.34`      [work/s10b-matched-u3.log],
    and a capture then showed a single advertiser, 10.38.22.34, with no
    checksum complaint                           [work/s11-matched-wire-u2.log].
    ** So it is the MISMATCH that breaks the pair, not either mode. **
  - Reverting both to the default re-converged again [work/s12c-reconverged-u3.log].

WHAT IS *NOT* ESTABLISHED, AND MUST NOT BE READ INTO THIS
  Which of the two modes is the RFC 5798 §5.2.8-conformant one is **NOT settled
  by this bench**. RFC 5798 requires the VRRPv3-over-IPv4 checksum to cover a
  pseudo-header, and the obvious reading of the A/B is that the validator
  implements that rule and the DUT's default does not -- but that reading rests
  on the validator being correct, which was not independently verified. The
  validator here is the **DUT's own bundled `tcpdump`**, so the measurable,
  assumption-free statement is:

    ** two components of the same firmware image disagree about the checksum of
       its own default VRRPv3 advertisement, and the vendor ships a command that
       makes them agree. **

  The interop question -- whether a third-party RFC-conformant VRRPv3 router
  would discard the default-mode advertisements and become a second master -- is
  **NOT MEASURED**. The instrument it needs is a third-party conformant VRRPv3
  peer, and this bench has none. It is stated as the open question, not as a
  result. What IS measured is that a receiver which rejects these frames ends up
  as a second master, because the mismatched-mode experiment produced exactly
  that outcome between two AW+ routers.

OPERATIONAL CAUTION THAT FOLLOWS, WHATEVER THE ANSWER
  `alternate-checksum-mode` must be set on EVERY router in a virtual router or
  on NONE. Setting it on one -- a staged upgrade, or a mixed-vendor rollout --
  silently yields two masters for one virtual IP. The guide does not say so:
  the VRRP Feature Overview and Configuration Guide C613-22049-00 REV E, read in
  full, contains **zero** occurrences of "alternate" and **zero** of "checksum".
  The command is undocumented there, and the DUT's own help for it is bare
  (`alternate-checksum-mode ?` -> `<cr>`, with only the one-line mode-list
  description "Enable alternate checksum mode for IPv4")  [work/s9-qmark-altcksum.log].

