================================================================================
FINDINGS
================================================================================
F-17 -- see the full section above. The default VRRPv3-for-IPv4 checksum is not
  the one the device's own tcpdump accepts, `alternate-checksum-mode` makes it
  accepted, and a pair with MISMATCHED modes produces two masters for one
  virtual IP, both answering ARP from the same virtual MAC. Same-vendor
  default-to-default interop is unaffected and was proven so. Wants a developer:
  it is a spec-conformance question, like F-14.

O-37 -- `circuit-failover` AND `transition-mode` ARE BOTH REFUSED WHILE THE VRRP
  SESSION IS ENABLED: `% You must disable this session first`. The guide's
  procedures do not mention the ordering, and a stop-on-error config driver
  abandons the rest of its batch at that line. The working order is
  `disable` -> the command -> `enable`. Same family as O-13 (a trigger's
  `description` before its `type`). `priority`, `virtual-ip` and `preempt-mode`
  do NOT need it.     [work/s13-cktfail-u2.log, work/s16-transition-u2.log]

O-38 -- THERE IS NO `version` COMMAND, SO "v3" CANNOT BE CONFIGURED OR READ BACK
  FROM THE CONFIGURATION. `version ?` in `config-router` mode answers
  `% Unrecognized command`, and the guide upgrades v2 to v3 by changing the
  software release and rebooting. Three things do establish v3, all of them
  measured: the advertisement's version field on the wire; the existence of
  `transition-mode` and `alternate-checksum-mode`; and `Accept mode: TRUE` by
  default, which the guide attributes specifically to the AW+ VRRPv3
  implementation. Grading "v3" from a config line is impossible here, and a
  tester who looks for one will wrongly report the feature missing.

O-39 -- `accept-mode` IS NOT AN IPv4 `router vrrp` COMMAND on this platform,
  although `show vrrp` reports `Accept mode: TRUE`. The 14-command router-vrrp
  list is advertisement-interval, alternate-checksum-mode, circuit-failover,
  disable, do, enable, exit, help, no, preempt-mode, priority, show,
  transition-mode, virtual-ip. Accept mode is on, documented as the VRRPv3
  default, and not configurable for IPv4. Likewise `vrrp vmac` is a GLOBAL
  command, not a router-mode one, and VMAC is already enabled by default
  (`show vrrp` -> `VMAC enabled`).

O-40 -- `no interface vlanN` CAN BE REFUSED WITH `% Removal of interface not
  allowed` once the SVI has no address and no member ports -- there is then
  nothing to remove, and deleting the VLAN from the database finishes the job.
  It succeeded on swi_a for group C and was refused on swi_b here, so a restore
  recipe must gate on the output rather than assume either behaviour. cfg.py
  stops at the `% ` and abandons the rest of the batch, which is how a teardown
  silently half-completes.

CONFIRMED, NOT NEW
  - x960.md 7A.1: `service vrrp` sent alone on each device; no rejection.
  - x960.md 7A.2 / O-24: both halves produced by ONE operation (teardown step 1).
  - x960.md 7B.8: `port1.0.48` read `Designated Discarding` on the first
    post-reload STP read and Forwarding on the second. The LEG V6 far-side count
    would have read as a persistence failure if measured in that window.
  - x960.md 6.5 (all SVIs share one MAC) is exactly why the virtual MAC is the
    only distinguishable address on this segment, and the V1 three-way ARP leg
    shows all three addresses side by side.
  - x960.md 7.9: reloads to `login:` at 60 s and 62 s, inside the 60-68 s range.
  - the staged-boot-config persistence recipe worked cleanly again, and
    startup-config and flash:/default.cfg were never written.

AUTHORED CLAIMS, ANSWERED
  (1) "configure a basic VRRP per the FOG document" -- done, and every default
      the guide states (priority 100, preempt true, master-down ~3x the
      interval, the master answering ARP with the virtual MAC, accept mode on
      for v3, INIT on interface down) was checked against the device.    PASS
  (2) "end device(s) can be reached when either the master or the backup router
      loses connection" -- BOTH halves measured in the dataplane with a control
      at zero and an instrument control at 5/5: backup down, 5/5 at ttl 63
      (LEG V3); master's monitored circuit down, 5/5 at ttl 62 through the new
      master (LEG V4).                                                   PASS
  (3) "role transitioning works correctly ... no unexpected errors, crashes" --
      Master -> Backup -> Master measured end to end, each transition confirmed
      on both devices and in the dataplane by the TTL. Neither device logged an
      error or restarted; both answered every console command throughout; the
      post-run probe is exit 0 with no advisories.                       PASS
      (The one anomalous state seen, the split master, was DELIBERATELY induced
      by a mismatched `alternate-checksum-mode` and is written up as F-17; it is
      not a spontaneous fault and it did not occur in any default-mode leg.)

VERDICT: PASS -- all three authored claims pass, graded on the wire. VRRPv3 read
off the advertisement's own version field with the virtual MAC 00:00:5e:00:01:0a
as the Ethernet source; the ARP reply for the virtual IP carries that MAC and the
same capture proves it is neither router's real address; the role transition
measured in the dataplane as TTL 63 -> 62 -> 63 on the virtual-MAC flow while the
instrument flow stayed at 63 and an unowned-MAC control stayed at 0; transition
mode produced paired v2 and v3 advertisements 26 us apart; the whole thing
survived a cold boot and was proven gone again after removal. Produced F-17 and
O-37..O-40. All three devices restored byte-identical, verified.
